            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groups"} topological groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            all the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+groups"} quotient groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi fin
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/N_{fin}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi fin
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_{fin}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+subgroup"} normal subgroup
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+of+a+subgroup"} index
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            induced from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroups"} subgroups
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Note that the profinite completion actually is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+group"} profinite group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \to \hat{G}
          %p
            More formally, we note that for any group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+category"} cofiltered category
            under inclusion. (Denote it by
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/N
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            gives a functor from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi G
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_G^{op}
            to the category of finite groups. It is thus a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+group"} profinite group
            in the sense given in that entry, i.e. a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-object"} pro-object
            in the category of finite groups. This pro-object is the profinite completion of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p The above topological version of this, with which we started, is obtained by means of the equivalence between the category of pro-(finite groups) and that of the groups internal to profinite spaces that is by taking the limit in the category of topological groups of the diagram of (discrete) finite groups that the above construction gives one.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The inclusion
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi inc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} inc
              from the category,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi FinGrps
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinGrps
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-left+adjoint"} pro-left adjoint
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-objects"} procategories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi pro
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo −
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mi inc
                    %mo :
                    %mi pro
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo −
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mi FinGrps
                    %mo →
                    %mi pro
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Grps
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    pro\!-\!inc : pro\!-\! FinGrps\to pro\!-\Grps
            %p
              and that functor
              %em does
              have a left adjoint. If we restrict that ‘pro-adjoint’ to the subcategory of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi pro
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo −
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mi FinGrps
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pro\!-\! FinGrps
              given by the ‘constant’ pro-objects, then the result is the pro-finite completion construction that is given above. Because of this, if we think of the natural functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi pro
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo −
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to pro\!-\! C
              to be an inclusion, i.e. think of an object as a pro-object indexed by the one arrow category, we can give a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
              for the pro-finite completion of a group
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              to finite groups, and just encodes the obvious fact that any homomorphism from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              to a finite group factors through one of its finite quotient groups. If we write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{G}
              for the pro-finite completion, the universal cone is a map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\to \hat{G}
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi pro
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mo −
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mi Grps
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pro\!-\! Grps
          %h3#pro_completion_of_a_group
            Pro-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            completion of a group
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            be any class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+groups"} finite groups
            that is closed under the formation of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroups"} subgroups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/images"} images
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+extensions"} group extensions
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em
                pro-
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                group
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+limit"} inverse limit
              of an inverse system of groups in the class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              or alternatively a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-object"} pro-object
              in the full subcategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              determined by the class
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            The subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pro
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo −
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mi FinGrps
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pro\!-\!FinGrps
            consisting of the pro-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            groups and the continuous homomorphisms between them will be denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi pro
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo −
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pro\!-\!\mathcal{C}
          %p This notation now has two definitions, but, as the corresponding categories are equivalent, this causes no problem.
          %p
            The categories of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pro
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo −
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pro\!-\!\mathcal{C}
            form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/varieties"} varieties
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi prof
                    %mo −
                    %mi FinGrps
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} prof-FinGrps
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variety"} variety
            in any algebraic context means a subcategory of ‘algebras’ closed under products, subobjects and quotients. We note the condition on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            implies the closure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            under finite products, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is what is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em pseudovariety
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pro
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo −
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mi FinGrps
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pro\!-\!FinGrps
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic"} monadic
            over the category of spaces. This means that free objects exist in all the
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi pro
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo −
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pro\!-\!\mathcal{C}
          %h2#examples Examples
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Consider the profinite completion of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
              of an complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+variety"} projective variety
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              has an underlying
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
              of loops
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                      %mi top
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1^{top}(X)
              can be defined in the usual way. But one can also define the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+fundamental+group"} algebraic fundamental group
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                        %mi alg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1^{alg}(X)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                        %mi top
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1^{top}(X)
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+completion+of+the+integers"} profinite completion of the integers
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo ^
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mo ←
                      %mi n
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi n
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\widehat {\mathbb{Z}} \coloneqq \underset{\leftarrow}{\lim}_n \mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+integers"} p-adic integers
              for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo ^
                    %mo ≃
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi p
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mi p
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\widehat{\mathbb{Z}} \simeq \underset{p}{\prod} \mathbb{Z}_p
                    \\,.
            %p
              For more on this see at
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+integers"} p-adic integers
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adele"} adele
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idele"} idele
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %h3#profinite_completion_of_spaces Profinite completion of spaces
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+completion+of+a+space"}> Profinite completion of a space
              \.
          %p (Beware there are two possible interpretations of this term. One is handled in the section above, being profinite completion of the homotopy type of a space. The entry linked to here treats another more purely topological concept.)
          %h3#equational_or_monadic_completions Equational or monadic completions
          %p Profinite completion of groups is a special case of a general process that ‘completes’ a category together with a ‘forgetful functor’ to some ‘base’ category, replacing it by a category which is equational/monadic over the base.
          %ul
            %li
              D. Gildenhuys and J. Kennison,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Equational completions, model induced triples and pro-objects
          %h3#profinite_rings Profinite rings
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+rings+are+profinite"} compact Hausdorff rings are profinite
          %h3#profinite_completions_of_homotopy_types Pro-finite completions of homotopy types.
          %p
            Artin and Mazur in their lecture note on étale homotopy introduced a process of profinite completion, generalising that for groups in as much as the profinite completion of an Eilenberg-Mac Lane space having
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as fundamental group has the profinite completion of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as
            %em its
            fundamental group. (WARNING: This needs a bit more detail to make it true! so this part of the entry needs more work.)
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                L. Ribes and P. Zalesskii, 2000,
                %em Profinite groups
                , volume 40 of
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Ergebnisse der Mathematik und ihrer Grenzgebiete
            %li
              %p
                J. Dixon, M. du Sautoy, A. Mann and D. Segal, 1999,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Analytic pro-p groups
